1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,400
Ghana is home to the world’s largest man-made lake.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:06,700
But there’s something about that record you might not know.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:09,900
Lake Volta is the largest by surface area.

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:13,400
It covers about 8,500 square kilometres.

5
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:17,600
Now, look at Lake Kariba, between Zambia and Zimbabwe.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:22,700
It covers a smaller area — about 5,600 square kilometres.

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,200
So Lake Volta must hold more water, right?

8
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:28,100
Not quite.

9
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:35,200
At full capacity, Lake Volta can hold about 148 cubic kilometres of water.

10
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:41,300
To put that into perspective, imagine those big 5,000-litre Polytanks you see around Ghana.

11
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,600
You’d need almost 30 billion of them to hold that much water.

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,600
Now, Lake Kariba?

13
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,400
At full capacity, it can hold about 181 cubic kilometres.

14
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:58,600
That’s more than 30 cubic kilometres more water, despite covering less surface area.

15
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:00,800
How is that possible?

16
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:02,900
Think of two containers.

17
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:06,200
One is very wide, but relatively shallow.

18
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,400
The other is narrower, but much deeper.

19
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:12,900
The wider container covers more surface area.

20
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:16,200
But the deeper one can hold more water.

21
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:19,300
And that’s essentially what’s happening with these two lakes.

22
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:23,800
Lake Volta spreads across a vast network of flooded river valleys.

23
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:26,900
Lake Kariba fills a deeper basin.

24
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:30,300
On average, it’s about one and a half times as deep.

25
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:36,400
So Lake Volta holds the record for the largest man-made lake by surface area.

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,500
But Lake Kariba has the greater water storage capacity.

27
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:42,600
Both are enormous.

28
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:45,200
They’re just enormous in different ways.

29
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:50,300
So the next time you hear that Ghana has the world’s largest man-made lake,

30
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:52,400
there’s one question worth asking.

31
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:54,300
Largest by what?

32
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:56,800
Unpacked by GilB.

33
00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:58,700
There’s more to the story.
